::ENG3_U02_L06_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when public rail lines are expanding into rural areas. Author A: The rail line will carry more than passengers. It will carry medicine, books, and letters to towns that have waited too long for connection. A community should not fear a road simply because it is made of iron. Author B: A rail line may bring letters, but it also brings buyers who see land only as numbers on paper. Before we cheer the engine, we should ask who owns the tracks and who must move when the price of progress rises. What shared topic do both authors address? {
~The cost of books.#Books are mentioned only as one possible benefit in Author A's passage.
=The expansion of rail lines into rural areas.#Correct. Both authors address the effects of rail expansion.
~The history of medicine.#Medicine is only one detail in Author A's reasoning.
~The design of train engines.#Engines are mentioned, but the main topic is rail expansion and its effects.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when public rail lines are expanding into rural areas. Author A: The rail line will carry more than passengers. It will carry medicine, books, and letters to towns that have waited too long for connection. A community should not fear a road simply because it is made of iron. Author B: A rail line may bring letters, but it also brings buyers who see land only as numbers on paper. Before we cheer the engine, we should ask who owns the tracks and who must move when the price of progress rises. How does Author A reason about the rail line? {
~Author A argues that rail lines should be feared because they are made of iron.#Author A says the community should not fear the rail line.
~Author A argues that land buyers are the main benefit.#That idea belongs closer to Author B's concern, not Author A's reasoning.
=Author A argues that the rail line will connect rural towns to useful goods and communication.#Correct. Author A lists medicine, books, and letters as benefits.
~Author A argues that no one should travel by rail.#The passage presents rail as beneficial.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when public rail lines are expanding into rural areas. Author A: The rail line will carry more than passengers. It will carry medicine, books, and letters to towns that have waited too long for connection. A community should not fear a road simply because it is made of iron. Author B: A rail line may bring letters, but it also brings buyers who see land only as numbers on paper. Before we cheer the engine, we should ask who owns the tracks and who must move when the price of progress rises. How does Author B reason about the rail line? {
=Author B warns that rail expansion may bring displacement and outside control.#Correct. Author B asks who owns the tracks and who must move.
~Author B celebrates the rail line as pure progress.#Author B is cautious, not celebratory.
~Author B focuses only on the beauty of engines.#The engine is mentioned as part of a warning about progress.
~Author B says letters should never be delivered.#Author B acknowledges letters but says that benefit is not the whole issue.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when public rail lines are expanding into rural areas. Author A: The rail line will carry more than passengers. It will carry medicine, books, and letters to towns that have waited too long for connection. A community should not fear a road simply because it is made of iron. Author B: A rail line may bring letters, but it also brings buyers who see land only as numbers on paper. Before we cheer the engine, we should ask who owns the tracks and who must move when the price of progress rises. What time-period concern connects both passages? {
~Whether paper is more valuable than land.#This is too narrow and not the shared concern.
~Whether rural areas should stop receiving letters.#Neither author argues this.
~Whether engines can be made from iron.#The iron detail is figurative in Author A's reasoning.
=Whether technological expansion helps or harms rural communities.#Correct. Both authors respond to the social effects of rail expansion.
}

::ENG3_U02_L06_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.R.11] Context: Both authors write during a time when public rail lines are expanding into rural areas. Author A: The rail line will carry more than passengers. It will carry medicine, books, and letters to towns that have waited too long for connection. A community should not fear a road simply because it is made of iron. Author B: A rail line may bring letters, but it also brings buyers who see land only as numbers on paper. Before we cheer the engine, we should ask who owns the tracks and who must move when the price of progress rises. Which comparison is most accurate? {
~Both authors reject rail expansion for the same reason.#Author A supports rail expansion while Author B questions its costs.
=Author A emphasizes connection and access, while Author B emphasizes ownership and displacement.#Correct. This comparison captures both authors' reasoning.
~Author A discusses land prices, while Author B discusses medicine.#These details are reversed or inaccurate.
~Both authors focus mainly on train speed.#Neither passage focuses mainly on speed.
}
